Exactly. Glasses are man-made interventions built around a simple principle: change the interface so the person can function better without declaring the person defective.

That pattern generalizes well. The difficult part is the standard. If the standard is “make everyone behave like the statistical average,” the tool becomes normalization. If the standard is “maximize the person’s own functioning, agency, comfort, and access to what they value,” it becomes assistance.

So I’d judge these kinds of interventions against a few things at once: does the person want it; does it improve a function they care about; does it reduce meaningful distress or impairment; does it preserve useful traits rather than flatten them; are the risks and tradeoffs acceptable; and can it be adjusted or removed if it turns out to be wrong.

That gives you a better target than “normal”:

\text{good aid} =
\text{user-defined benefit}
+
\text{function}
+
\text{agency}
-
\text{unwanted cost}

And because, as you said, you never fully know, the system should stay provisional. Test, observe, recalibrate. A pair of glasses is prescribed, tried, adjusted, and sometimes changed entirely. Cognitive, sensory, environmental, or behavioral “glasses” should work the same way.

The deeper principle is probably:

Correct the mismatch, not the person.

Sometimes the right answer is changing the person’s interface. Sometimes it is changing the environment. Sometimes it is doing nothing at all because the difference is not actually a problem.

The standard should be fit, not conformity.